Possible DoS on server after 502 error /ws/subscribe


## Investigation (2026-07-23, grok) — see finding

Finding: `.collab/findings/2026-07-23T08-05-00Z-b270-ws-subscribe-dos-after-502.md`

### Live evidence
- Production relay.c2c.im currently crash-looping: SIGSEGV status=139 every ~4–8 min on deployed v0.14.1 git=e4a988e (B219 fix IS in that SHA — residual crash remains). Edge appears as 502/timeout.
- Local `c2c relay subscribe-daemon` (pid 3838462, ~1d16h old) while origin hung:
  - 464 threads, ~531 MB RSS, FDSize 1024
  - ESTAB TCP to CF edge grew 105 → 193 in 10s (FD 596 → 693) — active reconnect storm
  - ~495 Unix IPC clients on relay-subscribe.sock

### Root model
502 (from residual SIGSEGV) primes the loop; unmetered `/ws/subscribe` + no client handshake timeout + short reconnect backoff amplify into a client/server DoS feedback loop.

### Code gaps
1. Server: `/ws/subscribe` NOT in `Relay_ratelimit.classify_endpoint` (unmetered). No max concurrent subscribers.
2. Client: `Relay_ws_client.connect_subscribe` has no connect/handshake timeout; hung edge holds ESTAB forever.
3. Client: subscribe-daemon backoff 1s→30s, no jitter; resets to 1s on any success; multi-alias = multi-storm.
4. Cancel/in-flight connect may leak FDs under `Lwt.pick` cancel.

### Fix order
1. Client: handshake timeout + jittered backoff + in-flight connect cap + cancel-safe close (local, no Railway).
2. Server: rate-limit + subscriber cap on `/ws/subscribe` (relay deploy).
3. Separate: re-open residual SIGSEGV post-B219 (pull core on e4a988e).

### Ops
Kill/restart runaway pid 3838462 when ready: `c2c relay subscribe-daemon shutdown`.


## Decomposed bugs (from investigation)

| ID | Scope | Pri |
|----|--------|-----|
| **B271** | Residual SIGSEGV post-B219 (502 primer) | critical |
| **B272** | Client: hard timeout on connect+handshake | high |
| **B273** | Client: jittered backoff + stable-session reset + circuit breaker | high |
| **B274** | Client: cancel mid-connect must close FDs | high |
| **B275** | Client: cap concurrent in-flight connects | high |
| **B276** | Server: meter `/ws/subscribe` (was unmetered) | high |
| **B277** | Server: cap concurrent subscribers / per-IP upgrades | high |
| **B278** | Ops: `list` is per-IPC-client only (footgun) | medium |
| **B279** | **Proper end-to-end rate limiting** (server policy + client Retry-After; depends B276/B277) | high |

B270 remains the umbrella / incident narrative; close when the DoS feedback loop is broken (client storm controls + server meter/caps + no residual crash preferred).


## Umbrella glue shipped (2026-07-23)

Not duplicating B271–B278 concrete fixes. B270 adds operator/visibility glue:

1. **Doctor warning** — `check_id=relay.subscribe_daemon_storm`
   - Pure: `Relay_doctor.classify_subscribe_daemon_storm` + `subscribe_daemon_storm_check`
   - Live: samples `~/.c2c/relay-subscribe.sock.pid` + `/proc/<pid>/{fd,status}`
   - Fail when metrics match 2026-07-23 incident (FD≥400 / socket_fds≥100 / threads≥200 / rss≥300MB)
   - Inconclusive when elevated; omitted when quiet or no daemon
2. **Hermetic tests** — `ocaml/test/test_c2c_doctor_capabilities.ml` group `B270 subscribe-daemon storm` (4 cases, including locked B271–B279 map)
3. **Public runbook** — `docs/relay-subscribe-daemon.md` reconnect-storm section + mitigations table

